  </p>

  <h2>Research</h2>
  <p>
    My work uses first-principles calculations to link the atomic structure of
    materials to their properties. Before my PhD, I studied the stability and
    reactivity of NiCo<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> and NiFe-LDH catalysts for the
    oxygen evolution reaction in alkaline conditions, including surface
    energies and adsorption of reaction intermediates. I have also computed the
    thermoelastic properties of the Ti<sub>2</sub>AlC MAX phase, and
    contributed to the implementation of hybrid exchange-correlation
    functionals in the SIESTA code.
  </p>

  <h2>Background</h2>
  <p>
    I completed my MSc in Physics (Materials Science, Computation and
    Condensed Matter Theory) and my BTech in Technical and Applied Physics at
    The Technical University of Kenya, where I later worked as a research
    assistant and mentored undergraduate projects. I have also trained with
    the SIESTA community through the East African School on DFT and the CECAM
    "First steps with SIESTA" school.
  </p>

  <h2>Tools I use</h2>
  <p>
    SIESTA, Quantum ESPRESSO, VASP, Phonopy, Phono3py, BoltzTraP, Python, and
    Linux-based high-performance computing environments.
  </p>
